# Generated userprogram test executables. `build.sh` assembles and links
# these from the `.s` sources sitting next to them, and the kernel embeds
# them via `include_bytes!`, so they are build outputs, not sources. The
# `.s` files themselves, the linker scripts, and real fixtures (e.g.
# `userprog/classes.dex`) ARE tracked.
source/nykernel/userprog/*.elf
source/nykernel/userprog/*.exe
source/nykernel/userprog/*.dll

# Rust build output (also matched by the repo-root `target/` rule; kept
# here so the kernel tree is self-describing if ever split out).
source/nykernel/target/

# minus the UEFI stub's generated state (same policy as the kernel
# target/ rule): cargo output, the assembled ESP image / VARS store, and
# the EDK2 OVMF .fd firmware blobs (external x86-64 firmware binaries,
# not project sources -- obtain them from an EDK2/edk2-ovmf release;
# every boot transcript in IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md names the exact
# file names the uefi/tools/ directory needs).
source/nykernel/uefi/target/
source/nykernel/uefi/dist/
source/nykernel/uefi/tools/
# The generated digest build.sh records for the stub's Stage-2 kernel
# image integrity check (a build-path fact, never source).
source/nykernel/uefi/src/kernel.sha256

# The ADR-0014 chain's AUTHENTICATED half, its generated state:
# tools/make_carrier.sh signs the kernel image just linked into the
# stub's include_bytes!-ed carrier and records the anchor's DER next
# to it. Both regenerate on every build (the signer's dev key and
# certificate live under uefi/tools/trust/, covered by the
# uefi/tools/ rule), so neither is source by the same policy as the
# digest line above.
source/nykernel/uefi/src/carrier.der
source/nykernel/uefi/src/trusted-root.der
# ...and the seven negative carriers make_carrier.sh generates beside
# them (one per refusal shape main.rs's negative controls pin, the
# five chain shapes plus the two validity-window ones).
source/nykernel/uefi/src/carrier-neg-*.der
# ...the MOK path's control carrier (the generated alternate chain,
# same generated-state policy as carrier.der itself).
source/nykernel/uefi/src/mok-carrier.der
# ...and the five generated dbx revocation-control blobs (the
# signature-list shape the revocation walk's every-boot controls
# run against) plus the two generated MokList blobs (the
# enrollment + stranger shapes the MOK path's controls run
# against), regenerated beside the carriers on every build.
source/nykernel/uefi/src/dbx-neg-*.der
source/nykernel/uefi/src/moklist-*.der
